VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: T6
ATTEMPT: 2
---
ATTACK 1 (falsifiability of the rescoped README sentence) — SUCCEEDED, this is the deciding failure.

README:84 now reads: "The files you keep in `data/` need to be real files, not symlinks.
SimpleBudget saves by writing a fresh file and renaming it into place, so when it saves
over a symlink, the link itself is replaced with a regular file and the file the link
pointed to stops being updated."

This is still an unconditional claim about how SimpleBudget's live save mechanism behaves
for files under data/. Sweeping internal/ and cmd/ (excluding _test.go) for os.WriteFile /
os.Create / O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC|O_APPEND finds a LIVE, wired counterexample the worker did not
address:

  internal/handlers/backup/handlers.go:642-681  func HandlePlotly(w, r)
    cachePath := filepath.Join(cfg.DataDirectory, "cache", "plotly.min.js")   // L643
    ...
    if err := os.WriteFile(cachePath, data, 0644); err != nil {               // L677
    (bare os.WriteFile: O_WRONLY|O_CREATE|O_TRUNC, no O_EXCL, no staging file, no rename)

  cmd/server/main.go:251
    r.Get("/static/vendor/plotly.min.js", backup.HandlePlotly)

This is not a dead/test-only function like SaveUserSettings — it is routed to a live HTTP
GET endpoint in the running server (confirmed by grep on cmd/server/main.go, and exercised
by TestHandlePlotlyCDNFetchSuccess in handlers_test.go, which mocks the CDN and asserts the
200 response written to this exact path). cachePath is literally under cfg.DataDirectory,
which config.go defines as `<wd>/data` (config.go:85 `DataDirectory: filepath.Join(wd,
"data")`) — i.e. it IS "the files you keep in `data/`" the README sentence is about, not an
out-of-scope directory.

Reproduction (run in scratchpad, mirrors what HandlePlotly does on a cache miss):
  $ mkdir -p symtest/data/cache
  $ echo "original target content" > realtarget.js
  $ ln -s "$PWD/realtarget.js" data/cache/plotly.min.js
  $ cat repro.go   # os.WriteFile("data/cache/plotly.min.js", []byte("NEW CDN BYTES"), 0644)
  $ go run repro.go
  before: mode= Lrwxrwxrwx
  after:  mode= Lrwxrwxrwx  is symlink still? true
  $ cat realtarget.js
  NEW CDN BYTES

Result: the symlink at the data/-relative path SURVIVES (still Lrwxrwxrwx afterward), and
the file the link pointed to (realtarget.js) DOES receive the new bytes and keeps being
updated. This is the EXACT INVERSE of the README's new sentence: "the link itself is
replaced with a regular file and the file the link pointed to stops being updated." The
same category of bug that failed attempt 1 (SaveUserSettings) still lives on a genuinely
live, wired, tested code path that attempt 2 did not touch, rescope around, or even
mention. The manifest for T6.2 only lists README.md and internal/config/config.go as
changed (plus the pre-existing storage.go comment) — HandlePlotly was not examined.

Consequence for the rest of the review, given attack 1 already fails the task:

ATTACK 2 (accuracy of the new SaveUserSettings comment) — the comment's individual claims
check out in isolation (bare os.WriteFile follows symlinks: confirmed by the same repro
above; atomicWrite really does stage-then-rename: confirmed by reading
internal/services/storage/storage.go's atomicWrite body, temp file + os.Rename; no call
sites outside tests: `grep -rn SaveUserSettings --include=*.go` outside config.go itself
hits only internal/config/config_test.go). But an accurate comment on one bypassing
function does not rescue an unconditional README claim that a second, live, bypassing
function (HandlePlotly's cache write) falsifies.

ATTACK 3 (docs-only discipline) — PASSED on its own terms: `git diff HEAD` shows only
README.md, internal/config/config.go, internal/services/storage/storage.go, and
.swarm/ledger.tsv changed; the config.go and storage.go diffs are comment-only (no code
token changed); gofmt -l on both changed .go files produced no output; `go build ./...`
and `go vet ./...` are clean. This attack failed to find a violation, but it does not
offset the falsified claim above.

ATTACK 4 (wording traps: dangling symlink, symlinked directory) — not reached as a
deciding factor since attack 1 already establishes FAIL; noting for the record that the
sentence's "the file the link pointed to stops being updated" wording was not re-tested
against the directory-symlink case because the primary counterexample already stands.

CONCLUSION: The README's rescoped sentence is still a general, falsifiable claim about
SimpleBudget's save behavior for data/, and it is false for at least one live, wired,
tested call path (HandlePlotly's plotly.min.js cache write), reproduced above. Attempt 2
narrowed the doc's wording around the SaveUserSettings bug found in attempt 1 but did not
sweep for other live bypasses of the same class, and one exists. FAIL.
